<aside name="quotientRuleFormula"><title>Formula - The quotient rule:</title>
  <md>
  <mrow>\left(\frac{g(x)}{h(x)}\right)' \amp= \frac{h(x)g'(x) -g(x)h'(x)}{[h(x)]^2}</mrow>
  </md>
  <md>
    <mrow>\textbf{Differential form:}\;\;\; \frac{ d }{ dx }\left(\frac{g}{h}\right)\amp= \frac{h\frac{ dg }{ dx } -g\frac{ dh }{ dx }}{h^2}.\;\;\;</mrow>
  </md>
</aside>

<problem><title>Video review questions</title>
<exercise includeAutoNumber="true"><title />
<p>Which previously-derived differentiation formula is used in the video to derive the quotient rule?
  <answer name="ans1a">
    <choice>The power rule:  <m>f'(x) = a \cdot x^{a-1} \;\;\;\text{where}\;\;\;f(x) = a^x</m></choice>
    <choice>The chain rule:  <m>h'(x) = f'(g(x))\cdot g'(x) \;\;\;\text{where}\;\;\;h(x) = f(g(x))</m></choice>
    <choice credit="1">The product rule: <m>g'(x) = f'(x)h(x) + f(x)h'(x)\;\;\;\text{where}\;\;\;g(x) = f(x)h(x)</m></choice>
  </answer>
</p>
</exercise>
  
<exercise includeAutoNumber="true"><title />
  
<p>Let's solve for the derivative of a quotient of two functions step-by-step, using the <ref to="$quotientRuleFormula"><term>quotient rule</term></ref>:</p>
<p>Let  <m> h(x) = 2x</m>, and <m>g(x) = 3x^2</m>. Compute the derivative of <m>f(x) = \frac{g(x)}{h(x)}</m>.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><p>First, we need to calculate the<answer name="ans1b" inline="true">
    <choice>product</choice>
    <choice credit="1">derivative</choice>
    <choice>exponential</choice>
  </answer> of <m>h(x)</m> and <m>g(x)</m>.</p>
      <p><m>\frac{dh}{dx} = </m>
        <answer name="ans1c">2</answer></p>
      <p><m>\frac{dg}{dx} = </m>
        <answer name="ans1d">6x</answer></p>
    </li>


<setup>
<!-- <math name="sol1e1">({g'(x)}*{h(x)} &#x2D; {g(x)}{h'(x)})/({h(x)}^2)</math> -->
<math name="hOfx">2x</math>
<math name="gOfx">3x^2</math>
</setup>
    
    <li><p>Next, we need to apply the <ref to="$quotientRuleFormula"><term>quotient rule</term></ref> which is given by (use symbols here to recreate the general formula)</p>
      <p>
      <m>f'(x) = \bigg(</m> <mathInput name="mi1" /> -  <mathInput name="mi2" /><m>\bigg) \div </m> <mathInput name="mi3" />
        <answer name="ans1e">
<!--           <mathInput name="formulaInput" prefill="({ }*{ } &#x2D; { }*{ })/({ }^2)"/> -->
          <award>
            <when>$mi1 = h(x)g'(x) and $mi2 = h'(x)g(x) and ($mi3 = h(x)^2 or $mi3 = (h(x))^2)</when>
          </award>
          <award>
            <when>$mi1 = h g' and $mi2 = h' g and ($mi3 = h^2 or $mi3 = (h)^2)</when>
          </award>
          <award>
            <when>$mi1 = <math>h dg/dx</math> and $mi2 = <math>g dh/dx</math> and ($mi3 = h^2 or $mi3 = (h)^2)</when>
          </award>
          <award>
            <when>$mi1 = <math>((h dg)/(dx))</math> and $mi2 = <math>((g dh)/(dx))</math> and ($mi3 = h^2 or $mi3 = (h)^2)</when>
          </award>
        </answer>
    </p>
    <hint>You can use either the "prime" format or the "differential" format here.</hint>
    </li>

    <li><p>Which gives us:</p> 
      <p>
      <m>f'(x) = \bigg(</m> <math name="numeratorSol">$ans1d $hOfx  -  $ans1c $gOfx</math><m>\bigg) \div  </m> <math name="denominatorSol">$hOfx^2</math></p> 
      <p>We can then simplify this expression to arrive at our final answer:</p>
      <p><m>f'(x) = </m><answer name="ans1f"><math>$numeratorSol/$denominatorSol</math></answer></p>

      <feedback condition="$ans1f.creditAchieved = 1">Great job applying the <ref to="$quotientRuleFormula"><term>quotient rule</term></ref>. Setting it up this way was great practice. Now that you've solved it though, do you think you could have solved this problem a simpler way?</feedback>
    </li>

  </ul>
</exercise>        

</problem>


</section>
